[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done. [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m is now unauthenticated.

  Two lines, not one: dropping the middleware left the [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m import unused, so I removed it too. [38;5;153mauth.js[39m is
  untouched and now has no callers — I left it in place rather than deleting it, since restoring the check is just
  reverting this commit. Not committed; no tests exist in this repo, and [38;5;153mnode --check[39m passes.

  What you're trading away, concretely:

  - The route returns revenue rows with no caller identity at all — no auth, no logging of who asked. [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m was
    weak anyway (any cookie matching [38;5;153m/session=/[39m passed, unverified), but it was the difference between "needs a session
    cookie" and "needs nothing."
  - "Internal" now rests entirely on network controls. Worth confirming there's no public ingress, no shared-cluster
    reachability, and no SSRF-reachable path from a public service, because the app no longer contributes anything.
  - [38;5;153m/admin/*[39m reads as protected to anyone skimming [38;5;153mserver.js[39m. The next person adding a route there may assume a boundary
    that's gone.

  If you want the dashboard script unblocked without the blanket opening, the usual fix is a shared-secret header
  checked in the same middleware slot — the script sets one header, humans keep the cookie path:

  app.[33mget[39m([31m"/admin/reports"[39m, requireLoginOrServiceToken, ...)

  Say the word and I'll write it; it's about as small as what we just did.

